  "meta": {
    "title": "Cut boxes into labelled pieces",
    "description": "`splitBox` CUTS every cell's box along one axis into an ordered list of LABELLED PIECES, one point per piece — the split of a split grammar, written as sizes instead of corners. A CELL IS A POINT WITH A BOX: the standard `boundsMin`/`boundsMax`. Here three blocks stand in a row, each 8 wide and as tall as its own storey count, drawn once per block. A FIRST CELL IS MADE AT A POINT: `blocks` is setBounds with `anchor` \"baseCenter\" and `size` vec(8, 1.5 + 3 * n, 0.5) — the plinth's 1 and the cornice's 0.5 plus n storeys of 3, n = 2 + floor(3 * randomField(\"storeys\")), two to four — so each block STANDS on its row point, centred on it in x and z with its min y at P. The box nodes read a cell's box and never its P, and every point a sampler emits carries the zero box at the origin, so without a box at P every block would start from there. THE PIECES ARE THREE PARALLEL LISTS, one entry per segment from the min side: `labels`, `sizes` and `units`. `storeys` cuts each block in y into a plinth of 1 (\"absolute\"), as many storeys of 3 as the rest holds (\"repeat\", so a block 13.5 tall gets four) and a cornice of 0.5 (\"absolute\" again — absolute segments are taken first wherever they sit). A NESTED SPLIT IS ONE MORE NODE: `bays` cuts only the storeys in x — `where` is attributeIs(\"piece\", \"storey\"), so the plinth and the cornice pass through untouched, label and seed and all. Bays aim at 2.5 wide; 8 holds three of 2.67, and `snap` puts the two interior cuts on a 0.5 lattice, so they come out 2.5, 3 and 2.5 while the block's own ends never move. A SIZE CAN BE A FIELD, resolved on the PARENT cell, with one component per segment: `opening` cuts each bay in y into wall, opening and wall with `sizes` = vec(sill, height, 1). On the ground storey's middle bay the sill is 0 and the opening 2.4 — that is the door — and A PIECE OF ZERO SIZE IS DROPPED, so the door has no wall under it and no branch was written to say so. The last segment is \"relative\" with weight 1: it takes whatever the others leave, which is how a split says \"and the rest\". `jambs` cuts only the openings in x, 1 : 2 : 1 by weight, so each window and door is the middle half of its bay. Each piece keeps every attribute its cell carried — `level` (the storey's slot, written by the first split as its `indexAttr`) and `door` reach the pieces three splits down — and is seeded hashCombine(cellSeed, slot), exactly as repeatPoints seeds copy k, so this graph and a repeatPoints + setBounds spelling of it draw the same numbers. The pieces tile their blocks exactly, so the last nodes only draw them. `tint` colours every piece by its label. `inset` is an offsetBox that moves every piece's four x and y faces in by 0.04 so the cuts show, and an opening's front and back in by 0.4 of its depth, read BY NAME as box(\"sz\") — the piece's z span, the boundsMin/boundsMax expression it stands for and nothing more — so the opening is a fifth of the wall deep and reads as a recess. `boxesToMesh` then builds every piece's box as faces where it stands, carrying its colour.",
    "tags": [
      "basics",
      "split",
      "bounds",
      "grammar"
    ]
  },
